# Every user-facing change prepends one entry to the top of the CHANGELOG array in
# src/data/changelog.js, so any two open PRs would otherwise ALWAYS conflict there —
# only the first merges clean; the rest go "dirty" and auto-merge.yml skips them
# (see CLAUDE.md ▸ "A stalled PR is yours to finish"). `merge=union` tells git to
# keep BOTH sides' added lines when merging/rebasing this file instead of raising a
# conflict, so both new entries land in the array (valid JS; the only cost is that
# same-day order may shuffle — cosmetic, and the data test pins only date order).
# This is the standard fix for changelog merge conflicts and is honored by GitHub's
# server-side merge, so concurrent PRs stop colliding on the changelog.
#
# CAVEAT — union has one corruption mode, so the result is always verified. When two
# branches both insert at the TOP of the array, their hunks end on the identical line
# `  ] },`; git treats that as shared trailing CONTEXT rather than part of either
# insertion, and union emits it ONCE. The entry that sorts first is then left with an
# unclosed object literal and the file stops parsing. auto-merge.yml runs
# `tools/changelog-lint.js` after its union merge and BEFORE pushing, so a collapse
# fails loudly instead of shipping. Writing an entry on ONE line (as ~216 entries here
# already do) sidesteps it entirely — a whole entry shares no trailing text with its
# neighbours, so there is nothing for union to fold.
src/data/changelog.js merge=union
